<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-5-3-division-algorithm" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Division Algorithm</title>

  <p>
    When working with relatively small numbers, determining whether or not <m>d \mid n</m> is not a difficult task. But what if you were asked to determine if <m>27,182 \mid 314,159</m>? How would you do it?
  </p>
  
  <p>
    One way is list all of the multiples of the divisor until you meet or exceed the dividend. In our case: 
  </p>

  <p>
    <md number="no">
      <mrow> 27,182 \cdot \phantom{0} 1 \amp = 27,182 </mrow>
      <mrow> 27,182 \cdot \phantom{0} 2 \amp = 54,364 </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp \vdots </mrow>
      <mrow> 27,182 \cdot 11 \amp = 299,002 </mrow>
      <mrow> 27,182 \cdot 12 \amp = 326,184 </mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    In the penultimate step, the product was smaller than our target, but in the final step, the product exceeded it. That means that:
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>27,182 \cdot 11 \lt 314,159 \lt 27,182 \cdot 12.</md>
  </p>

  <p>
    But we can do better. Notice that:
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>314,159 - 27,182 \cdot 11 = 15,751.</md>
  </p>

  <p>
    This allows us to write:
  </p>

  <p>
    <md>314,159 = 11 \cdot 27,182 + 5,751.</md>
  </p>

  <p>
    In this context, <m>11</m> is known as the <em>quotient</em> and <m>5,751</m> is known as the <em>remainder</em> when <m>314,159</m> is divided by <m>5,751</m>. Note that this is the same quotient and remainder you would have gotten if you used the standard division-with-remainder process you learned when you were younger.
  </p>

  <p>
    We formalize this idea below.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-div-alg">
    <title> The Division Algorithm </title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>n, d</m> be integers with <m>d > 0</m>. Then, there exist unique integers <m>q</m> and <m>r</m>, with <m>0 \leq r \lt d</m>, such that <m>n = qd + r</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
      <p>Omitted, but the intuition above is the important takeaway.</p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
  <definition xml:id="def-div-alg">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the context of <xref ref="thm-div-alg"/>, <m>d</m> is called the <em>divisor</em>, <m>n</m> is called the <em>dividend</em>, <m>q</m> is called the <em>quotient</em>, and <m>r</m> is called the <em>remainder</em>. We write:
      </p>

      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow> q \amp = n \text{ div } d </mrow>
          <mrow> r \amp = n \text{ mod } d </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="ex-div-alg">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the quotient and remainder when:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p><m> 22</m> is divided by <m>7</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>-22</m> is divided by <m>7</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <hint>
      <p>
        Remember, <m>0 \leq r \lt d</m> by definition!
      </p>
    </hint>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>We write <m>22 = 3 \cdot 7 + 1</m>, and so <m>22 \text{ div } 7 = 3</m> and <m>22 \text{ mod } 7 = 1</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>We write <m>-22 = (-4) \cdot 7 + 6</m>, and so <m>-22 \text{ div } 7 = -4</m> and <m>-22 \text{ mod } 7 = 6</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    The solution to part 2 may have been counterintuitive to you. Your instict may have been to write <m>-22 = (-3) \cdot 7 - 1</m>, similar to part (a). While this is perfectly valid, mathematically, this does not satisfy the condition in <xref ref="thm-div-alg"/> that <m>0 \leq r \lt d</m>. The remainder, under these conventions, is <em>always</em> nonnegative. This convention may seem arbitrary (and in some sense, it is), but it gives us a very nice property: for a fixed integer <m>d</m>, the function <m>f: \mathbb{Z} \to \{0, 1, \ldots, d\}</m> such that <m>f(n) = n \text{ mod } d</m> generates a <em>partition</em> of the integers. We will explore this in the next section. 
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-mod-practice">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If today is Tuesday, what day will it be 1000 days from today?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The day of the week repeats every seven days. We're not really interested in <em>how many times</em> we cycle through those seven days, we just need to know  how many days we go over on the very last cycle. Said another way, we need to find the remainder when 1000 is divided by 7.
      </p>

      <p>
        Note that <m>1000 = 142 \cdot 7 + 6</m>. Therefore, 1000 days after today occurs 142 weeks and 6 days from today. Six days after Tuesday is Monday, so if today is Tuesday, then it will be a Monday in 1000 days.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>